¶
'''wiel†''', ''n.'' See ''weel''<sup>1</sup>.

'''wield''' (wēld), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''welden'' (pret. ''welde'', ''walde'', ''welte'', ''welded'', ''weldide'', pp. ''welt''), < AS. ''geweldan'', ''gewyldan'', have power over; a secondary form of the strong verb, ME. ''walden'', ''wealden'' (pret. ''wield''), < AS. ''wealdan'' (pret. ''weóld'', pp. ''wealden''), have power over, govern, rule, possess, = OS. ''waldan'' = OFries. ''walda'' = D. ''welden'' = OHG. ''waltan'', dispose, manage, rule, MHG. G. ''walten'', rule, = Icel. ''valda'', wield, = Sw. ''välla'' (for *''välda''), occasion, cause, = Dan. ''volde'', commonly ''for-volde'', occasion, cause, = Goth. ''waldan'', govern; cf. Russ. ''vladieti'', reign, rule, possess, make use of, = Lith. ''waldyti'', rule, govern, possess; prob. < L. ''valere'', be strong, have power: see ''valid''.] '''1.''' To have power or sway over; rule; govern; manage.
{{smaller block|<poem>Now coronyd is the kyng this cuntre to ''weld'';
Hade homage of all men, & honour full grete,
And began for to gouerne, as gome in his owne.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 5381.}}}}
{{smaller block|Adam . . . ''welte'' al Paradys, saving o tree.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Monk’s Tale, l. 20.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thence to the famous orators repair,
Those ancient, whose resistless eloquence
''Wielded'' at will that fierce democratie,
Shook the arsenal, and fulmined over Greece.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., iv. 269.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Where’er that Power may move . . .
Which ''wields'' the world with never-wearied love.</poem>}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Shelley'', Adonais, xlii.}}}}
'''2.''' To use or exert in governing; sway.
{{smaller block|Her new-born power was ''wielded'' at the first by unprincipled and ambitious men.{{right|''De Quincey.''}}}}
'''3.''' Hence, in general, to exercise; put to practical or active use, as a means, an instrument, or a weapon; use with freedom and ease: as, to ''wield'' a hammer.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ac his witt ''welt'' he after as wel as to-fore.</poem>{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 142.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>In oure chapitre praye we day and nyght
To Crist that he thee sende heele and myght
Thy body for to ''weelden'' hastily.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', Summoner’s Tale, l. 239.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Part ''wield'' their arms, part curb the foaming steed.</poem>{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xi. 643.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A potent wand doth Sorrow ''wield''.</poem>{{right|''Wordsworth'', Peter Bell.}}}}
'''4†.''' To have; possess; enjoy.
{{smaller block|And sum prince axide him, seyinge, Good maister, what thing doynge schal I ''welde'' euerlastyng lyf?{{right|''Wyclif'', Luke xviii. 18.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And alway [he] slewe the kynges dere,
And ''welt'' them at his wyll.</poem>{{right|''Lytell Geste of Robyn Hode'' (Child’s Ballads, V. 108).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>But tell me, that hast seen him, Menaphon,
What stature ''wields'' he, and what personage?</poem>{{right|''Marlowe'', Tamburlaine, I., ii. 1.}}}}
'''To wield a good baton.''' {{smaller|See ''baton''.}}

'''wield†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''welde'' (cf. ''walde'', ''wolde'', < AS. ''geweald'', power); from the verb.] Command; power; management.
{{smaller block|Doo weel bi hem of thi good that thou hast in ''welde''.{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 43.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
ful bonds of wedlock; a man’s spouse: the correlative of ''husband.''

{{smaller block|<poem>He ȝede forth bliue
To Rymenhild his ''wyue.''</poem>
{{right|''King Horn'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|The Soudan hathe 4 ''Wyfes,'' on Cristene and 3 Sarazines; of the whiche on dwellethe at Jerusalem, and another at Damasce, and another at Ascalon.
{{right|''Mandeville,'' Travels, p. 38.}}}}

{{smaller block|A good ''wife'' is heaven’s last best gift to man, his angel and minister of graces innumerable, his gem of many virtues, his casket of jewels. {{right|''Jer. Taylor.''}}}}

'''All the world and his wife.''' {{smaller|See ''world.''}}—'''Auld wives’ tongues.''' {{smaller|See ''auld.''}}—'''Deceased Wife’s Sister Bill.''' {{smaller|See ''bill''<sup>3</sup>.}}—'''Dutch wife.''' {{smaller|See ''Dutch.''}}—'''Inhibition against a wife.''' {{smaller|See ''inhibition.''}}—'''Old wife.''' {{smaller|See ''old.''}}—'''Old wives’ tale.''' {{smaller|See ''tale''<sup>1</sup>.}}—'''Plural wives,''' {{smaller|consorts or concubines of the same man under a polygamous union.}}—'''Ratification by a wife.''' {{smaller|See ''ratification.''}}—'''Wife’s equity,''' {{smaller|in ''law,'' the general rule established by courts of equity that where a husband resorted to a court of equity to enforce his common law marital right to take his wife’s property, that court would, in general, oblige him to make a reasonable provision out of the fund for the benefit of his wife and children. This doctrine has been extended or superseded by acts which secure the whole property of a wife to herself.}}

¶
'''wife†''' (wīf), ''v. i.'' [< ''wife, n.''] To take a wife; marry.

{{smaller block|''Eu.'' . . . An’t you weary of ''wifeing?''
''Po.'' I am so weary of it that, if this Eighth should die to Day I would marry the Ninth to-Morrow.
{{right|''N. Bailey,'' tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, I. 348.}}}}

'''wife-bound''' (wīf′bound), ''a.'' Devoted or tied down to a wife; wife-ridden. [Rare.]

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|A ''wife-bound'' man now dost thou rear the walls<br>Of high Carthage? {{right|''Surrey'', Æneid, iv. 343.}}}}

¶
'''wife-carl''' (wīf′kärl), ''n.'' A man who busies himself about household affairs or woman’s work. [Scotch.]

'''wifehood''' (wīf′hu̇d), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wifhod'', ''wiifhood'', < AS. ''wīfhād'', < ''wīf'', wife, + ''hād'', condition.] Wifely character or condition; the state of being a wife.
{{smaller block|<poem>She taughte al the craft of fyn lovinge,
And namely of ''wyfhood'' the livinge.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 545.}}}}
{{smaller block|The stately flower of female fortitude,<br>Of perfect ''wifehood.'' {{right|''Tennyson'', Isabel.}}}}

'''wifekin†''' (wīf′kin), ''n.'' [ME., < ''wife'' + ''kin''<sup>1</sup>.] Womankind. ''Genesis and Exodus'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 656.

'''wifeless''' (wīf′les), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wiifles'', ''wyfles'', ''wyflees''; < ''wife'' + ''-less''.] Without a wife; unmarried.
{{smaller block|Sixty yeer a ''wyflees'' man was he.{{right|''Chaucer'', Merchant’s Tale, l. 4.}}}}

'''wifelike''' (wīf′līk), ''a.'' [< ''wife'' + ''-like''.] Resembling or pertaining to a wife or woman.
{{smaller block|''Wifelike'' government. {{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., ii. 4. 138.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Wifelike'', her hand in one of his.{{right|''Tennyson'', Aylmer’s Field.}}}}

'''wifely''' (wīf′li), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wifly'', ''wifli'', < AS. ''wīflīc'', < ''wīf'', wife + ''-līc'', E. ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] Pertaining to or befitting a wife; like a wife.
{{smaller block|<poem>Yit is it bet for me
For to be deed in ''wyfly'' honestee
Than be a traitour living in my shame.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 2701.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|With all the tenderness of ''wifely'' love.
{{right|''Dryden'', Amphitryon, iii.}}}}

¶
'''wife-ridden''' (wīf′rid″n), ''a.'' Unduly influenced by a wife; ruled or tyrannized over by a wife; henpecked.
{{smaller block|Listen not to those sages who advise you always to scorn the counsel of a woman, and if you comply with her requests pronounce you ''wife-ridden.'' {{right|''Mrs. Piozzi.''}}}}

'''wiflet†''', ''n.'' [Origin obscure.] A kind of ax.
{{smaller block|xj. crosbowes whereof iij. of stele, and v wyndas. Item, j. borespere. Item, vj. ''wifles.'' {{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 487.}}}}

'''wifman†''', ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''woman.''

'''wig<sup>1</sup>†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''wig'', < AS. ''wicg'' = Icel. ''viggr'' (''viggja-''), also ''vigg'', a horse, steed; connected with AS. ''wegan'', carry: see ''way''<sup>1</sup>, ''weigh''<sup>1</sup>.] A beast of burden, as a horse or an ass.
{{smaller block|Ac theh he [were] alre louerdes louerd, and alre kingene ki[n]g, natheles he sende after the alre unwurtheste ''wig'' one to riden, and that is asse.
{{right|''Old Eng. Homilies'', 2d ser., p. 89.}}}}

'''wig<sup>2</sup>''' (wig), ''n.'' [Also ''wigg'' (and erroneously ''whig''); early mod. E. ''wygge''; = D. ''wig'', ''wigge'', a wedge, = G. ''weck'', ''wecke'', a sort of bread: see ''wedge''<sup>1</sup>.] A sort of cake. [Obsolete or local.]
{{smaller block|Home to the only Lenten supper I have had of ''wiggs'' and ale. {{right|''Pepys'', Diary, II. 117.}}}}
{{smaller block|You may make ''wigs'' of the biscuit dough, by adding . . . currans. {{right|''Coll. of Receipts'', p. 2. (''Jamieson.'')}}}}

