¶
'''wig'''<sup>3</sup> (wig), ''n.'' [Abbr. of ''periwig'': see ''periwig''
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Forms of Wig worn in Great Britain in the 17th and 18th centuries.}}}}
{{c|{{smaller|1, Time of James I.; 2, time of Charles I.; 3, 4, 5, Restoration, Charles II.; 6, 7, time of James II. and Anne; 8, 9, time of William and Mary; 10, campaign wig, 1684; 11, Ramilie wig, 1736; 12, bob-wig, 1742; 13, 14, the Macaronis’ wig, 1772; 15, 16, wigs of 1774-80; 17, 18, wigs of 1785-95.}}}}
and ''peruke''.] '''1.''' An artificial covering of hair for the head, used generally to conceal baldness, but formerly worn as a fashionable head-dress. {{smaller|Wigs are usually made to imitate the natural hair, but formal curled wigs are worn as part of their professional costume by judges and lawyers in Great Britain. Wigs are much used on the stage. See ''peruke''.}}
{{smaller block|I have often wanted him to throw off his great flaxen ''wig''; . . . with his usual Gothic vivacity, he said I only wanted . . . to convert it into a tête for my own wearing.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', She Stoops to Conquer, ii.}}
I never believe anything that a lawyer says when he has a ''wig'' on his head and a fee in his hand.
{{right|''Trollope'', Phineas Redux, lxi.}}}}
'''2.''' The full-grown male fur-seal of Alaska, ''Callorhinus ursinus''. See cut ununder ''fur-seal''.—'''3.''' The head. [Colloq.]—'''Allonge wig.'''
